A ball of mass $m$ bounces off the Earth or a wall of mass $M$, with incoming and outgoing $y$-momenta $p_y$ and $q_y$, and $Q_y$ the momentum of the Earth or wall after the collision. By conservation of total momentum,
$
  p_y = q_y + Q_y.
$
By conservation of energy,
$
  (p_y^2)/(2 m) = (q_y^2)/(2 m) + (Q_y^2)/(2 M).
$
Substituting the first equation into the second gives
$
  (1)/(2 m) (q_y^2 + 2 q_y Q_y + Q_y^2) & = (q_y^2)/(2 m) + (Q_y^2)/(2 M) \
  (1)/(2 m) (2 q_y Q_y + Q_y^2) & = (Q_y^2)/(2 M).
$
If $M >> m$, then $Q_y^2/(2 M) approx 0$, so
$
  (Q_y)/(2 m) (2 q_y + Q_y) & approx 0 \
  Q_y & approx -2 q_y \
  p_y & approx - q_y.
$
Hence the angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence: $beta = alpha$.
